# Line endings, pinned at the root, because the product's text lives in raw string literals.
#
# Every word the app ships — the mission file it writes for the agent, the schema text, the guide —
# is a C# `"""` raw string literal, and a raw string literal keeps the line endings of the SOURCE
# FILE. So a checkout that writes `.cs` with CRLF (the hosted windows-latest runner's default
# `core.autocrlf=true`) compiles a different program: every line of that text gains a trailing `\r`,
# and anything that compares it line by line stops matching. That is what went red on CI run
# 34073713557, windows-latest only, with macos and ubuntu green on the same tree.
#
# `text=auto` alone does NOT fix it: it normalises to LF in the repository but still hands the
# working tree whatever the platform asks for. `eol=lf` is what pins the bytes on disk, on every
# platform, so the compiler sees one program everywhere.

# Normalise everything in the repository to LF; the lines below decide the working tree.
* text=auto

# Source and anything the product's text or the build reads. LF everywhere, no exceptions.
*.cs        text eol=lf
*.csproj    text eol=lf
*.props     text eol=lf
*.sln       text eol=lf
*.slnx      text eol=lf
*.md        text eol=lf
*.json      text eol=lf
*.yml       text eol=lf
*.yaml      text eol=lf
*.sh        text eol=lf
*.py        text eol=lf
*.txt       text eol=lf
*.strategy  text eol=lf

# Windows-only scripts, where CRLF is what the interpreter and the convention expect.
*.cmd       text eol=crlf
*.bat       text eol=crlf
*.ps1       text eol=crlf

# Binaries: none are tracked today. Listed so the first one added is not corrupted by normalisation.
*.png       -text
*.jpg       -text
*.jpeg      -text
*.gif       -text
*.ico       -text
*.pdf       -text
*.zip       -text
*.exe       -text
*.dll       -text
*.snk       -text
*.ttf       -text
*.otf       -text

# Vendor material stays byte-for-byte as the vendor shipped it: no normalisation, no diff noise.
docs/atas-api-*.txt -text
